ng that  he was drunk or something about effect  in other words to a large extent for  many things we can be what we want to be  and in particular this is true in  language learning now I don't believe as  I've said many times that some people  have a natural ability for languages I  mean to some extent that may exist but  it's not decisive it's not crucial I  don't believe in this business of and  and it's typically the people who don't  put the effort or put the time and I  shouldn't say effort the time into  learning a language who don't try who  say well I don't have an ear for it I  don't believe it I don't believe that  all Swedes and all Singaporeans have an  ear and an ability for language which  let's say Americans don't have I just  don't believe that that is part of the  genetic makeup of any people what does  happen is that the cultural ego of  Swedes and Singaporeans maybe less they  don't necessarily see themselves  primarily as part of Swedish culture  they're quite happy to absorb other  cultures and I see this when I lived I  saw this when I lived in Japan those  North Americans I was a small number  mind you but who were happy to mix in  with the Japanese who wanted to do  things Japanese who weren't hung up  about being American they learned to  speak and they learn to speak very well  so cultural weightlessness extremely  important more important certainly than  the teachers more important than the  grammar it's the willingness to see  yourself as part of that other culture  and and we see this i mean it's  play-acting we see how actors I've seen  American actors who i mean i'm a native  speaker of english and i thought they  were British I thought they were from  London and they because actors are so  good at projecting themselves into the  role that they're playing they're no  longer who they are in real life they  are the role that they're playing and we  have to have that kind of an attitude  when we go to learn a language  comfortably  when 