herefore he was Cato he was  always Cato because they couldn't get  out of that sense that if it's k ay t 0  therefore you know everything has to be  pronounced the way it's written in my  own line  you have to leave that behind I often  think too that East Asians you know  they're so concerned about their own  uniqueness all the time the number of  times in Japan people would say well  Japan must be the most difficult  language in the world as well no not  necessarily the Koreans are the same way  and the Chinese very disappointed if you  tell them that in fact their language is  not so difficult in fact all kinds of  people go there and if they have the  right attitude they get right in there  and they learn to speak with very little  accent so much so that that they appear  on local television they become a bit of  a freak show here's this foreigner who's  done the amazing impossible thing of  learning to speak Japanese or Chinese  very very well not a big deal if you  want to do it if you're willing to take  yourself away from your native culture  and project yourself into the culture of  that other group you know in Reverse I  think as I say East Asian sometimes have  trouble with that because they're so  determined to be unique you know our  history is unique everything is unique I  mean when you talk to Chinese people  they talked about their 5,000 years of  history as if that's some kind of fact  well it's a bit of a digression but  there is nothing particularly important  that happened in China 5,000 years ago  that that somehow is the starting point  of China there was no China then there  is an oral history of the Shah dynasty  which is 4,000 years old so we could  talk about the beginning of a dynasty in  a very small part of what is today China  in a very smart which eventually  expanded and expanded and expanded to  become today's China but that was four  thousand not five thousand and there are  artifacts and archeological diggings  that go well beyond five thousand years  as there are in Mesopotamia in Turkey in  Central America along the Indus Valley  in Crete in Europe and so forth so  there's nothing unique there but to them  it's very important 5,000 years of  history and therefore and the more  they're hung up in the uniqueness of  their own culture the less able they are  to project themselves into this other  language and that makes them less  capable language learners I don't say  that blanket all East Asians are less  capable language learners but those that  are successful from those  countries are the ones that are able to  leave behind their culture that doesn't  mean that you leave it behind  permanently doesn't mean that you stop  being who you are in fact learning  another language often makes you more  fond of your own culture but it means  that while you are learning the language  and while you are speaking the language  you're able to project yourself you're  able to pretend you're able to play act  you're able to abandon and you're not  waving your flag you are another person  so cultural weightlessness the ability  to project into another language  extremely important for language  learning I look forward to your comments
 

